From: programista-zacny <5969291+programista-zacny@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] cpuset: keep the tasks of a cpuset whose CPUs are all offline (cpuset_v2_mode)

When the last CPU of a cpuset goes offline, 3.10 moves the tasks of that
cpuset to the nearest ancestor that still has CPUs, and they stay there:
Android would find its top-app or background processes in the root cpuset
with no trace of where they belong. On this board every cpuset that does
not contain cpu0 gets into that state each time the screen goes off,
because the hotplug governor then leaves cpu0 alone online.

For a hierarchy mounted with cpuset_v2_mode, do what the option stands for
upstream:
- A cpuset that has requested CPUs but none of them online keeps its
  tasks. They run on the CPUs of the nearest ancestor that has some, and
  return to their own CPUs as soon as one of those is online again.
- A task can be attached to such a cpuset (it gets the ancestor's CPUs),
  and "cpus" of a cpuset with tasks can be set to CPUs that are all
  offline. Both still fail with ENOSPC when no CPUs are requested at all.
- New read-only file "effective_cpus": the CPUs the tasks of the cpuset
  run on at the moment, while "cpus" shows the requested ones.

Mechanics: effective_cpumask_cpuset() finds the cpuset whose cpus_allowed
applies; update_tasks_cpumask() and cpuset_cpus_allowed_fallback() use it.
The hotplug work no longer empties a v2-mode cpuset that is only out of
CPUs, and it re-evaluates the tasks of such a cpuset on every hotplug
event, since the ancestor's CPUs may have changed. cpuset_attach() and
sched_setaffinity() already walk up through guarantee_online_cpus().

Hierarchies mounted without the option keep the 3.10 rules (tasks are
moved out, ENOSPC on attach). The only change there: tasks of a cpuset
that has just lost its last CPU get the ancestor's CPUs right away instead
of keeping a mask of offline CPUs until they are moved.

Derived from upstream:
  b8d1b8ee93df "cpuset: Allow v2 behavior in v1 cgroup" (v4.14), the
               behaviour
  070b57fcacc9 "cpuset: introduce effective_{cpumask|nodemask}_cpuset()"
  5c5cc62321d9 "cpuset: allow to keep tasks in empty cpusets"
  88fa523bff29 "cpuset: allow to move tasks to empty cpusets" (v3.11),
               the way it is done on a cpuset.c without effective_cpus

Adapted for 3.10:
- 3.10 has cpus_allowed and (from the restore patch) cpus_requested where
  upstream has effective_cpus and cpus_allowed. A cpuset's own effective
  mask is cpus_allowed here; only the empty case borrows from an ancestor.
- Memory nodes are left alone: a cpuset without memory nodes still loses
  its tasks. This board has one node.
- Descendants that borrow CPUs from a cpuset whose "cpus" is rewritten are
  refreshed through their hotplug work items instead of upstream's
  update_tasks_cpumask_hier(): that walk drops the RCU lock around
  cgroup_scan_tasks(), which the 3.10 cgroup iterators do not survive.
- The option changes nothing else that upstream ties to it (no
  hierarchical effective masks, sched domain partitioning untouched).

Compile-tested as part of the cpuset series with CONFIG_CPUSETS=y: full
zImage with the GCC 4.9 arm-linux-androideabi prebuilt, and
kernel/cpuset.o and kernel/cgroup.o at every step of the series; no
warnings in the files touched. Not run on the device.

[combined set, 2026-11-06] Header only, the diff is the staged one. The
paragraph above said "five patches". The series was staged as five
(staging/backport-cpusets 0014-0018) and compiled step by step in that
form; the combined set has four of them, 0015-0018. The fifth, "cpuset:
let CAP_SYS_NICE move other users' tasks into a cpuset" (staged 0015),
is replaced by "cgroup: one credential rule for moving another user's
task" (0014). The four were compiled again in that form, as one zImage
build of the whole combined series, not step by step.
---
 kernel/cpuset.c | 132 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 116 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)

diff --git a/kernel/cpuset.c b/kernel/cpuset.c
index abda98a..6e3a067 100644
--- a/kernel/cpuset.c
+++ b/kernel/cpuset.c
@@ -128,6 +128,44 @@ static inline struct cpuset *parent_cs(const struct cpuset *cs)
 	return NULL;
 }
 
+/*
+ * A hierarchy mounted with "cpuset_v2_mode" treats CPU hotplug the way
+ * cgroup v2 does: a cpuset keeps its tasks while none of the CPUs it
+ * requested is online.  Until one comes back they run on the CPUs of the
+ * nearest ancestor that has some, see effective_cpumask_cpuset().  Without
+ * the option such tasks are moved to that ancestor for good.
+ */
+static inline bool is_in_v2_mode(const struct cpuset *cs)
+{
+	return cs->css.cgroup->root->flags & CGRP_ROOT_CPUSET_V2_MODE;
+}
+
+/*
+ * effective_cpumask_cpuset - the cpuset whose cpus the tasks of @cs run on
+ * @cs: the cpuset of interest
+ *
+ * That is @cs itself, or its nearest ancestor with cpus if @cs has none at
+ * the moment.  top_cpuset tracks cpu_active_mask and is never empty, so the
+ * walk always ends.
+ */
+static struct cpuset *effective_cpumask_cpuset(struct cpuset *cs)
+{
+	while (cpumask_empty(cs->cpus_allowed))
+		cs = parent_cs(cs);
+	return cs;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Can tasks stay in @cs?  Not without memory, and outside v2 mode not
+ * without cpus either.
+ */
+static bool cpuset_must_be_vacated(const struct cpuset *cs)
+{
+	if (nodes_empty(cs->mems_allowed))
+		return true;
+	return cpumask_empty(cs->cpus_allowed) && !is_in_v2_mode(cs);
+}
+
 #ifdef CONFIG_NUMA
 static inline bool task_has_mempolicy(struct task_struct *task)
 {
@@ -490,13 +528,17 @@ static int validate_change(const struct cpuset *cur, const struct cpuset *trial)
 
 	/*
 	 * Cpusets with tasks - existing or newly being attached - can't
-	 * have empty cpus_allowed or mems_allowed.
+	 * have empty cpus_allowed or mems_allowed.  In v2 mode the cpus
+	 * only have to be requested, they need not be online.
 	 */
 	ret = -ENOSPC;
-	if ((cgroup_task_count(cur->css.cgroup) || cur->attach_in_progress) &&
-	    (cpumask_empty(trial->cpus_allowed) ||
-	     nodes_empty(trial->mems_allowed)))
-		goto out;
+	if (cgroup_task_count(cur->css.cgroup) || cur->attach_in_progress) {
+		if (cpumask_empty(is_in_v2_mode(cur) ? trial->cpus_requested :
+						       trial->cpus_allowed))
+			goto out;
+		if (nodes_empty(trial->mems_allowed))
+			goto out;
+	}
 
 	ret = 0;
 out:
@@ -814,13 +856,14 @@ void rebuild_sched_domains(void)
  * Call with cpuset_mutex held.  May take callback_mutex during call.
  * Called for each task in a cgroup by cgroup_scan_tasks().
  * Return nonzero if this tasks's cpus_allowed mask should be changed (in other
- * words, if its mask is not equal to its cpuset's mask).
+ * words, if its mask is not equal to its cpuset's effective mask).
  */
 static int cpuset_test_cpumask(struct task_struct *tsk,
 			       struct cgroup_scanner *scan)
 {
-	return !cpumask_equal(&tsk->cpus_allowed,
-			(cgroup_cs(scan->cg))->cpus_allowed);
+	struct cpuset *cpus_cs = effective_cpumask_cpuset(cgroup_cs(scan->cg));
+
+	return !cpumask_equal(&tsk->cpus_allowed, cpus_cs->cpus_allowed);
 }
 
 /**
@@ -829,7 +872,8 @@ static int cpuset_test_cpumask(struct task_struct *tsk,
  * @scan: struct cgroup_scanner containing the cgroup of the task
  *
  * Called by cgroup_scan_tasks() for each task in a cgroup whose
- * cpus_allowed mask needs to be changed.
+ * cpus_allowed mask needs to be changed.  A cpuset that has no cpus at the
+ * moment hands out the cpus of its nearest ancestor that has some.
  *
  * We don't need to re-check for the cgroup/cpuset membership, since we're
  * holding cpuset_mutex at this point.
@@ -837,7 +881,9 @@ static int cpuset_test_cpumask(struct task_struct *tsk,
 static void cpuset_change_cpumask(struct task_struct *tsk,
 				  struct cgroup_scanner *scan)
 {
-	set_cpus_allowed_ptr(tsk, ((cgroup_cs(scan->cg))->cpus_allowed));
+	struct cpuset *cpus_cs = effective_cpumask_cpuset(cgroup_cs(scan->cg));
+
+	set_cpus_allowed_ptr(tsk, cpus_cs->cpus_allowed);
 }
 
 /**
@@ -931,6 +977,21 @@ static int update_cpumask(struct cpuset *cs, struct cpuset *trialcs,
 
 	heap_free(&heap);
 
+	/*
+	 * Descendants without cpus of their own run on @cs's cpus in v2
+	 * mode.  Their hotplug work re-evaluates them; it cannot be done
+	 * here, cgroup_scan_tasks() sleeps and the walk is under RCU.
+	 */
+	if (is_in_v2_mode(cs)) {
+		struct cpuset *cp;
+		struct cgroup *pos_cgrp;
+
+		rcu_read_lock();
+		cpuset_for_each_descendant_pre(cp, pos_cgrp, cs)
+			schedule_cpuset_propagate_hotplug(cp);
+		rcu_read_unlock();
+	}
+
 	if (is_load_balanced)
 		rebuild_sched_domains_locked();
 	return 0;
@@ -1399,8 +1460,15 @@ static int cpuset_can_attach(struct cgroup *cgrp, struct cgroup_taskset *tset)
 
 	mutex_lock(&cpuset_mutex);
 
+	/*
+	 * No tasks in a cpuset without cpus or mems.  In v2 mode it is enough
+	 * that cpus are requested; cpuset_attach() falls back to an ancestor's
+	 * while they are offline.
+	 */
 	ret = -ENOSPC;
-	if (cpumask_empty(cs->cpus_allowed) || nodes_empty(cs->mems_allowed))
+	if (cpumask_empty(is_in_v2_mode(cs) ? cs->cpus_requested :
+					      cs->cpus_allowed) ||
+	    nodes_empty(cs->mems_allowed))
 		goto out_unlock;
 
 	cgroup_taskset_for_each(task, cgrp, tset) {
@@ -1514,8 +1582,10 @@ static void cpuset_attach(struct cgroup *cgrp, struct cgroup_taskset *tset)
 	 * We may have raced with CPU/memory hotunplug.  Trigger hotplug
 	 * propagation if @cs doesn't have any CPU or memory.  It will move
 	 * the newly added tasks to the nearest parent which can execute.
+	 * (Not for a v2-mode cpuset that is only out of cpus: its tasks
+	 * stay, and got an ancestor's cpus above.)
 	 */
-	if (cpumask_empty(cs->cpus_allowed) || nodes_empty(cs->mems_allowed))
+	if (cpuset_must_be_vacated(cs))
 		schedule_cpuset_propagate_hotplug(cs);
 
 	mutex_unlock(&cpuset_mutex);
@@ -1527,6 +1597,7 @@ typedef enum {
 	FILE_MEMORY_MIGRATE,
 	FILE_CPULIST,
 	FILE_MEMLIST,
+	FILE_EFFECTIVE_CPULIST,
 	FILE_CPU_EXCLUSIVE,
 	FILE_MEM_EXCLUSIVE,
 	FILE_MEM_HARDWALL,
@@ -1689,6 +1760,18 @@ static size_t cpuset_sprintf_cpulist(char *page, struct cpuset *cs)
 	return count;
 }
 
+static size_t cpuset_sprintf_effective_cpulist(char *page, struct cpuset *cs)
+{
+	size_t count;
+
+	mutex_lock(&callback_mutex);
+	count = cpulist_scnprintf(page, PAGE_SIZE,
+				  effective_cpumask_cpuset(cs)->cpus_allowed);
+	mutex_unlock(&callback_mutex);
+
+	return count;
+}
+
 static size_t cpuset_sprintf_memlist(char *page, struct cpuset *cs)
 {
 	size_t count;
@@ -1724,6 +1807,9 @@ static ssize_t cpuset_common_file_read(struct cgroup *cont,
 	case FILE_MEMLIST:
 		s += cpuset_sprintf_memlist(s, cs);
 		break;
+	case FILE_EFFECTIVE_CPULIST:
+		s += cpuset_sprintf_effective_cpulist(s, cs);
+		break;
 	default:
 		retval = -EINVAL;
 		goto out;
@@ -1804,6 +1890,12 @@ static struct cftype files[] = {
 		.private = FILE_MEMLIST,
 	},
 
+	{
+		.name = "effective_cpus",
+		.read = cpuset_common_file_read,
+		.private = FILE_EFFECTIVE_CPULIST,
+	},
+
 	{
 		.name = "cpu_exclusive",
 		.read_u64 = cpuset_read_u64,
@@ -2117,8 +2209,15 @@ static void cpuset_propagate_hotplug_workfn(struct work_struct *work)
 		update_tasks_nodemask(cs, &tmp_mems, NULL);
 	}
 
-	is_empty = cpumask_empty(cs->cpus_allowed) ||
-		nodes_empty(cs->mems_allowed);
+	/*
+	 * A v2-mode cpuset that is out of cpus keeps its tasks; they run on
+	 * the cpus of an ancestor, and those may have changed even if @cs's
+	 * own did not.
+	 */
+	if (cpumask_empty(cs->cpus_allowed) && is_in_v2_mode(cs))
+		update_tasks_cpumask(cs, NULL);
+
+	is_empty = cpuset_must_be_vacated(cs);
 
 	mutex_unlock(&cpuset_mutex);
 
@@ -2301,12 +2400,13 @@ void cpuset_cpus_allowed(struct task_struct *tsk, struct cpumask *pmask)
 
 void cpuset_cpus_allowed_fallback(struct task_struct *tsk)
 {
-	const struct cpuset *cs;
+	struct cpuset *cs;
 
 	rcu_read_lock();
 	cs = task_cs(tsk);
 	if (cs)
-		do_set_cpus_allowed(tsk, cs->cpus_allowed);
+		do_set_cpus_allowed(tsk,
+				    effective_cpumask_cpuset(cs)->cpus_allowed);
 	rcu_read_unlock();
 
 	/*
